WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:11.840
hi my name is Emily Cochran and I'm the
writer producer and supporting actress

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:17.480
for a film called the stolen the stolen
was shot in New Zealand and was funded

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:22.759
out of the UK Germany New Zealand
obviously and a little bit out of the

00:00:22.759 --> 00:00:28.099
States and it was a really really tricky
thing to put together financially so

00:00:28.099 --> 00:00:32.570
I've been asked by film courage to talk
to you about film funding so the first

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:36.829
thing I'd like to say as a creative
person who had to go into film funding

00:00:36.829 --> 00:00:42.170
to fund my own film was it is difficult. I can't
really express how much I dislike it I

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:46.700
really do I think if you're a creative
which you may very well be you might be

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:50.150
a director or a writer trying to get
your own project off the ground it's

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:54.020
probably the last thing you want to
think about and frankly it is a very

00:00:54.020 --> 00:01:00.760
very different skill set but let me see
if I can help you a bit

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:07.900
so the first thing to have a think about
is sales agents sales agents are crucial

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:13.630
to your financial package in fact
without a sales agent and without their

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:19.420
projections it's really hard to move
forward and to getting a film funded so

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:24.460
the reason for this is that investors
have very little security when they

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:28.720
invest in a film they can't really say
whether it will ever make anything

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.520
because it's an unknown quantity however
a sales agent may be able to offer

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:39.130
projections based upon similar films
they've sold or films with similar

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:43.600
actors in them and that kind of thing
and if they're respected it does give

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:49.210
the investor a lot of comfort so how
does a sales agent assess your project

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:53.289
well the first thing they look at is
obviously the script and they look at

00:01:53.289 --> 00:01:58.560
the genre and in some cases if it's for
instance a horror or a martial arts film

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.520
the genre itself and if it's well
written could be enough for them to give

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.140
projections upon because it doesn't
necessarily have to have any named

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:15.060
actors in it but generally sadly you do
need the actors and the actors are

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:22.599
really what they base their projections
on if I'm honest so it'll keep coming

00:02:22.599 --> 00:02:26.980
back to that the actors the actors the
actors who is in it it's like everything

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:33.970
goes backwards from the screen so how do
you get the actors it is a whole

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:39.640
different tutorial however what I would
say is let me give you some advice if

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:46.959
your grandmother's second cousins dog
knows Brad Pitt please get friendly with

00:02:46.959 --> 00:02:54.640
that dog and if you can get something
via the dog - Brad Pitt then do it

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:58.959
because it is so hard to attach name
Talent particularly when you're a

00:02:58.959 --> 00:03:02.560
first-timer
you need to pull out all the stops work

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:07.300
out all your connections no matter how
tenuous and give it a go my advice is

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:13.500
always be polite don't be too pushy some
actors are really responsive

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:18.540
to an outside connection others are not
and they really want you to go through

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:24.930
the agent the problem is the bigger the
actor the more blockage from the agent

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:29.520
because they're being offered so much
stuff and obviously the agent is

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:34.440
filtering and trying to get the best
deals and your film even though your

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:40.050
script may be really great for that
actor might be too risky you're a

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:46.160
first-timer they've never heard of you
there's lots and lots of reasons so

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:53.640
whatever you can do try and get that to
an actor maybe a different way if you

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:59.130
can run the back door the other advice
is just write really good parts just

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:03.720
think about that and maybe offer it to
an actor who doesn't normally play those

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:08.670
kind of roles and they might just find
it an interesting thing to do and take

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:12.720
it I would also advise going via
managers and the states because the

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:18.060
managers are looking much more at the
overall career of the actor as opposed

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:25.020
to just you know what they're making and
you know American agents are well pretty

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:30.720
hard-nosed and they will they would just
curl you out they are obligated to give

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:34.890
the script to the actor but they don't
have to give it to them for 12 months

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:39.030
and they could also say anything they
like while they pass it over so just

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:43.380
bear that in mind
so say you get to that point you've got

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:45.750
your actor
you've got your great script you've

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:50.270
maybe got your storyboards you've got
some professional team members and

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:55.580
you've got sales estimates what next

00:04:56.569 --> 00:05:03.110
you can begin to piece together your
film finance plan film finance can come

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:07.459
from all different sources and I would
advise really doing some research a lot

00:05:07.459 --> 00:05:11.270
of it is grunt work
so one of the easiest sources of film

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:17.599
funding it comes from government tax
breaks or tax credits or tax grants and

00:05:17.599 --> 00:05:23.990
they can be given just for shooting a
film in the area and this can be your

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:29.659
state if you're in America it can be an
in Canada it can be your town it can be

00:05:29.659 --> 00:05:34.849
your country and don't be scared of
going outside your own country as well

00:05:34.849 --> 00:05:42.020
because you might find that there are
more or higher rates of tax breaks

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:46.490
outside and if your film isn't too
low-budget

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:50.539
it could really work for you or you
could look at doing a co-production

00:05:50.539 --> 00:05:56.089
where you tap into a tax break in one
country and a tax break in another and

00:05:56.089 --> 00:06:01.729
they could fit together it begins to get
a little bit complicated paperwork wise

00:06:01.729 --> 00:06:06.499
because you have to do official
co-productions but sometimes you don't

00:06:06.499 --> 00:06:11.919
in the stolen we didn't we did an
unofficial co-production with Germany so

00:06:11.919 --> 00:06:19.119
it is possible that's the first thing I
would look there you can get between

00:06:19.119 --> 00:06:27.349
twenty to forty percent of your budget
just from tax breaks so get online look

00:06:27.349 --> 00:06:31.729
at all of the Commission's everywhere
and see what they're offering in that

00:06:31.729 --> 00:06:38.719
area and if it means you have to move to
move your project to Australia or Canada

00:06:38.719 --> 00:06:46.039
or you know just Connecticut then do it
okay that will be the simplest thing to

00:06:46.039 --> 00:06:51.680
do obviously taking into account having
to hire a local crew and all that anyway

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:58.189
so that's the first thing the second
thing is there are grants often given

00:06:58.189 --> 00:07:04.129
separate grants so equity grants given
by film Commission's in Britain it's the

00:07:04.129 --> 00:07:08.089
BFI the British Film Institute in New
Zealand for instance it's the New

00:07:08.089 --> 00:07:11.479
Zealand Film Commission
and most countries have a Film

00:07:11.479 --> 00:07:16.339
Commission of some sort and you can
apply now this is really really

00:07:16.339 --> 00:07:20.319
difficult to get this money then
normally as a hell of a lot of criteria

00:07:20.319 --> 00:07:25.580
they have to take a lot of boxes because
it's government money so they have to

00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:31.489
have certain things in place before they
can give it to you and often it is

00:07:31.489 --> 00:07:38.509
leaning towards non-commercial projects
so if you have a commercial project it

00:07:38.509 --> 00:07:42.110
might be really difficult to get money
but I always say put in an application

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:46.879
and then forget about it if you get the
money from them and you get on to that

00:07:46.879 --> 00:07:52.849
system fantastic because once you're in
their system you can do really well out

00:07:52.849 --> 00:08:02.029
of it so the next thing is I get private
money there are lots of different

00:08:02.029 --> 00:08:08.509
options there are bank
well banking I guess is different than

00:08:08.509 --> 00:08:15.409
private but I would say bank gaps which
are difficult to get unless you have

00:08:15.409 --> 00:08:19.309
done pre-sales
and a lot of the first time is watching

00:08:19.309 --> 00:08:23.479
this will not get pre-sales pre-sales
have got more and more and more

00:08:23.479 --> 00:08:29.149
difficult to get and a lot of banks just
don't want to that they're incredibly

00:08:29.149 --> 00:08:33.469
risk-averse and they do not want to risk
not getting their money back so they

00:08:33.469 --> 00:08:38.959
like to be assured that when the film is
finished there will be a sale it's very

00:08:38.959 --> 00:08:43.519
hard to do at the moment so banks are
difficult but there are private

00:08:43.519 --> 00:08:47.360
companies that do gap
and when I say gap that is money that

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:52.939
comes out in first position
with nobody else okay so it's got to

00:08:52.939 --> 00:08:58.790
come out first and often that can be
between 10 and 20 percent so it's worth

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:03.709
that if you can get 20 for sure but
there'll be caveats

00:09:03.709 --> 00:09:10.879
again Google gap financing and see what
you find the same companies will

00:09:10.879 --> 00:09:14.870
probably if you are lucky enough to have
a presale they will also cashflow

00:09:14.870 --> 00:09:22.819
pre-sales moving on there is other types
of funding in Britain there are there is

00:09:22.819 --> 00:09:26.359
a
tax break for investors which is

00:09:26.359 --> 00:09:31.099
different from the government tax break
for filmmakers and that's called an

00:09:31.099 --> 00:09:37.399
enterprise investment scheme and that
means that the person investing gets a

00:09:37.399 --> 00:09:43.399
30%
well tax break on on their own tax bill

00:09:43.399 --> 00:09:47.539
and there could be similar things like
that and wherever you're living it's

00:09:47.539 --> 00:09:53.509
worth looking into this is a really good
thing because it is a risky business for

00:09:53.509 --> 00:09:57.679
a private investor to put money in so
that if they know that guaranteed some

00:09:57.679 --> 00:10:03.919
think back like a tax break it becomes a
little bit easier to convince them if

00:10:03.919 --> 00:10:09.019
you do tap into this and you're
fortunate enough to either find a fund

00:10:09.019 --> 00:10:13.819
that's already up and running and can
assess your project and put money and or

00:10:13.819 --> 00:10:20.209
you find a private individual who's
willing to do it you can really get a

00:10:20.209 --> 00:10:26.809
big chunk of your budget you know I I
would say 30% in my case I probably got

00:10:26.809 --> 00:10:32.349
close to 50% of my budget from private
individuals who went into an enterprise

00:10:32.349 --> 00:10:37.669
investment scheme and and a startup
Enterprise Investment Scheme which is

00:10:37.669 --> 00:10:44.449
another form of tax break which was
wonderful really really helpful the

00:10:44.449 --> 00:10:50.929
other forms of funding are there are
just straight equity funds out there

00:10:50.929 --> 00:10:58.249
many in America there are a few and the
UK and there's quite a few in Germany as

00:10:58.249 --> 00:11:08.089
well their money has come from venture
capitalists they've come from private

00:11:08.089 --> 00:11:12.919
individuals who just really like film
it's it's difficult but they are funds

00:11:12.919 --> 00:11:19.339
and you should just again Google
research really really research and look

00:11:19.339 --> 00:11:25.669
up film equity funds okay that's
different to film tax breaks it's

00:11:25.669 --> 00:11:31.959
different to gap funding because equity
is is a different animal they take a

00:11:31.959 --> 00:11:36.519
proportion of the movie

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:43.570
one of the other wonderful options if
you're lucky enough to meet them is a

00:11:43.570 --> 00:11:49.280
wealthy person now they're really hard
to find unless you're particularly

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:53.840
well-connected and if you're not well
connected but you know somebody who is

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:59.570
and they might be interested in in doing
film you know make a proposal to them

00:11:59.570 --> 00:12:04.970
say look do you think you could raise
fun funds for me on my movie for

00:12:04.970 --> 00:12:12.320
Commission for a credit whatever you
need to offer them and and we can become

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:18.280
a team it is worth finding these people
if you can and prefer ending them and

00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:22.490
really looking at a business deal the
only thing that I would say is that if

00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:27.230
you're talking to a private investor
really really be as honest as possible

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:32.180
about returns about the risk and all of
that because you don't want it to bite

00:12:32.180 --> 00:12:37.940
you on the ass later it would be a
nightmare and it's also really sad for

00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:42.110
the rest of us who are being honest
about whether the film's gonna make

00:12:42.110 --> 00:12:45.800
money or not because if they lose their
money they will never invest in a film

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:53.150
again so think about your future career
now a lot of wealthy people who may be

00:12:53.150 --> 00:12:57.860
wealthy from a different industry might
get involved with the film purely

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:02.630
because it's a film it's interesting
they might like the subject matter they

00:13:02.630 --> 00:13:06.950
may like the actor they might like yeah
the genre they might like where it's

00:13:06.950 --> 00:13:11.540
being shot it might be meaningful to
them in some way and it may not actually

00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:15.410
be about money
but ultimately you really want to try

00:13:15.410 --> 00:13:21.500
and get their money back and the other
point that I should make is that if you

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:27.410
do get a private individual try and
ensure that in the waterfall in their

00:13:27.410 --> 00:13:33.110
finance and the finance plan that they
come out in a reasonably high position

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:38.660
so they're not last on the list because
that that's not great so from a private

00:13:38.660 --> 00:13:42.200
individual you could get 10% of your
budget you could get more you could get

00:13:42.200 --> 00:13:47.090
five who knows but there are other
options which I will tell you about so

00:13:47.090 --> 00:13:51.079
the
option is to go and do a deal with a

00:13:51.079 --> 00:13:59.600
post-production house or a equipment
house and do in kind investment or they

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:06.709
may actually do physical cash investment
for a movie and in a way that's their

00:14:06.709 --> 00:14:14.120
way of discounting the their services
however it can come in in a version of

00:14:14.120 --> 00:14:20.750
an investment and that could give you up
to 5% of your budget also another little

00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:26.839
known and little utilized option is a
music deal so there are companies out

00:14:26.839 --> 00:14:31.420
there who will take the rights to your
soundtrack obviously it has to be

00:14:31.420 --> 00:14:39.529
original music and they will give you
money for it upfront or they might give

00:14:39.529 --> 00:14:44.899
it to you when you're in post often it's
only approximately 1% of your budget but

00:14:44.899 --> 00:14:48.829
it's really useful because it's cash
they don't come out in the waterfall

00:14:48.829 --> 00:14:54.730
they literally just take the the
soundtrack rights and then they go away

00:14:54.730 --> 00:15:02.300
so the other option which I've left to
last which the main reason being is is

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:08.180
that it is less and less likely these
days is a distribution deal so if you

00:15:08.180 --> 00:15:15.529
have a movie that is very attractive and
has a really well-known actor or it's a

00:15:15.529 --> 00:15:21.560
particular genre that's very hip and
that distributor believes that they can

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:27.560
make money out of it you make it a
pre-sale so they may commit to buying

00:15:27.560 --> 00:15:34.130
the movie before the film was made and
in that case you can then finance a

00:15:34.130 --> 00:15:41.000
foreign bank or a private investor and
that could be multi territory or it

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:45.589
could just be one territory you have to
be very careful about how many

00:15:45.589 --> 00:15:51.199
territories they take for what money
because if you've got other investors

00:15:51.199 --> 00:15:53.990
involved you have to be sure that
they're still getting a piece of the pie

00:15:53.990 --> 00:16:00.949
when they come out so those are the
options you need to get on Google you

00:16:00.949 --> 00:16:03.500
need to
start looking this stuff up and it's a

00:16:03.500 --> 00:16:09.250
lot of grunt work okay but I'm sure you
can do it

00:16:09.980 --> 00:16:15.590
so lastly remember film funding doesn't
stop once you've raised the money

00:16:15.590 --> 00:16:19.760
unfortunately
investors are gonna know what's going on

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:24.320
with their money all the way through the
process they're going to want reports

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:27.800
they're going to want to know what's
happening and boy when you have finished

00:16:27.800 --> 00:16:32.240
that film and it starts being shown to
buyers they're going to want to know

00:16:32.240 --> 00:16:36.620
what the results are and unfortunately
you're probably going to sweat because

00:16:36.620 --> 00:16:40.700
if they're not selling the film is not
selling for what you expected it to

00:16:40.700 --> 00:16:46.310
where it's not selling it can be really
stressful even though you may have

00:16:46.310 --> 00:16:53.030
warned the investors of the risks they
may not have really comprehended them or

00:16:53.030 --> 00:17:01.520
done they don't want to hear it and that
can be a lot of sweat so remember it

00:17:01.520 --> 00:17:05.839
doesn't stop this is why I said it sucks
because it doesn't matter that you raise

00:17:05.839 --> 00:17:10.130
the money and then you get to make the
film and you have that great time on set

00:17:10.130 --> 00:17:15.020
and you really enjoy everything and then
you get to show it to people you've

00:17:15.020 --> 00:17:21.770
still got people to answer to at the end
of it my final word is if you by any

00:17:21.770 --> 00:17:27.350
chance get offered by a studio to be
taken under their wing and they pay for

00:17:27.350 --> 00:17:32.870
the whole lot and just give you a fee
dear God take it okay just take it

00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:37.670
forget about the independent thing just
take the studio deal okay you don't want

00:17:37.670 --> 00:17:42.890
to be thinking about this stuff alright
really try and get away from it as

00:17:42.890 --> 00:17:48.710
quickly as you can so just think of this
smaller movie as your stepping stone to

00:17:48.710 --> 00:17:54.620
being looked after later if you can okay
now if any of you out there are thinking

00:17:54.620 --> 00:17:58.220
about funding your own film and you
think that I really know what I'm doing

00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:03.050
please don't come to me I'm not gonna
help you fund your film because I hate

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:07.660
it okay but watch the stolen

